WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.623
Picture a Saturday night, and a guest who says the food never came.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:14.252
The new manager runs into the kitchen, restarts the oven, resets the card reader, and moves two cooks to other stations.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.756
Ten minutes later, the soup arrives.

4
00:00:16.756 --> 00:00:19.111
Which of the three changes fixed it?

5
00:00:19.111 --> 00:00:19.962
Nobody knows.

6
00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:21.467
Maybe none of them did.

7
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:24.063
And next Saturday, it happens again.

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.358
Three changes and a happy ending isn't a diagnosis.

9
00:00:27.358 --> 00:00:30.022
It's a lottery ticket that happened to win.

10
00:00:30.022 --> 00:00:33.180
So when a Pod won't start, where do you look first?

11
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.205
Kubernetes makes guessing worse.

12
00:00:36.205 --> 00:00:38.334
A broken Pod is full of clues.

13
00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:40.392
The crashed container's logs.

14
00:00:40.392 --> 00:00:41.457
Its last state.

15
00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:42.735
The newest events.

16
00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:45.862
Now restart or delete it, to see if it helps.

17
00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:48.335
Kubernetes happily starts a fresh one.

18
00:00:48.335 --> 00:00:50.548
And the clues go with the old one.

19
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:51.995
So here's the rule.

20
00:00:51.995 --> 00:00:54.984
Read the evidence before you change anything.

21
00:00:54.984 --> 00:00:56.577
A restart deletes clues.

22
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:58.787
A method beats guessing.

23
00:00:58.787 --> 00:01:03.149
Work from the bottom up, because each layer depends on the one below it.

24
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.293
One, the Pod.

25
00:01:04.293 --> 00:01:07.539
Is it scheduled, running, ready, restarting?

26
00:01:07.539 --> 00:01:08.793
That's the table.

27
00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:10.039
Two, the Service.

28
00:01:10.039 --> 00:01:14.398
Does it select those Pods, and send to the port the app listens on?

29
00:01:14.398 --> 00:01:15.439
The host's list.

30
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.730
Three, the route.

31
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.786
Does the Gateway send traffic to that Service?

32
00:01:19.786 --> 00:01:20.783
The front door.

33
00:01:20.933 --> 00:01:23.352
And four, the node, the whole kitchen.

34
00:01:23.352 --> 00:01:26.088
It sits under every Pod, yet it comes last.

35
00:01:26.267 --> 00:01:26.914
Why last?

36
00:01:26.914 --> 00:01:30.872
A sick kitchen shows up as many tables failing at once.

37
00:01:30.872 --> 00:01:33.391
One broken Pod rarely points there.

38
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:39.085
At every layer, the commands are nearly always the same five.

39
00:01:39.085 --> 00:01:40.446
Get, for the status.

40
00:01:40.446 --> 00:01:43.780
Describe, reading the Events at the bottom first.

41
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:44.121
Logs.

42
00:01:44.121 --> 00:01:46.230
Events for the whole namespace.

43
00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.136
And then, tests from inside.

44
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.114
Down at the Pod, the trouble comes in two families.

45
00:01:52.114 --> 00:01:54.920
In the first, the container never ran at all.

46
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:57.451
So kubectl logs has nothing to show.

47
00:01:57.451 --> 00:02:01.291
The cook never lit the stove, so there's no smoke to read.

48
00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:03.940
The answer is in describe, under Events.

49
00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:07.531
In the second family, the container ran at least once.

50
00:02:07.531 --> 00:02:10.803
So it left clues in its logs, and in its last state.

51
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.265
Here's a classic.

52
00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.423
A Pod is in CrashLoopBackOff.

53
00:02:14.423 --> 00:02:16.953
kubectl logs shows nothing useful.

54
00:02:16.953 --> 00:02:18.888
Where's the error message?

55
00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:20.079
Pause and guess.

56
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.183
In the previous container's log.

57
00:02:24.183 --> 00:02:28.554
Plain logs shows the fresh container, which may have printed nothing yet.

58
00:02:28.554 --> 00:02:34.063
Dash dash previous shows the one that crashed: the note left by the cook who just went home.

59
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.369
And BackOff only means the kubelet waits longer between tries.

60
00:02:38.369 --> 00:02:39.781
It's never the cause.

61
00:02:40.533 --> 00:02:43.003
Let's run the method on a real mistake.

62
00:02:43.003 --> 00:02:46.676
Someone changed one setting for the app, and restarted it.

63
00:02:46.676 --> 00:02:48.639
Now the rollout never finishes.

64
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:49.775
Step one, get.

65
00:02:49.775 --> 00:02:52.980
One new Pod is Running, but zero of one ready.

66
00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:57.439
The three old Pods keep serving, so users see nothing wrong yet.

67
00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.988
The app counts visits in Redis.

68
00:02:59.988 --> 00:03:02.453
So the tempting move is obvious.

69
00:03:02.453 --> 00:03:03.532
Restart Redis!

70
00:03:03.532 --> 00:03:04.302
Let's not.

71
00:03:04.302 --> 00:03:05.612
Let's read first.

72
00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.442
Step two, the events.

73
00:03:07.442 --> 00:03:13.387
Readiness fails with 503, and the app returns 503 when it can't reach Redis.

74
00:03:13.387 --> 00:03:16.359
But Redis itself is Running and ready.

75
00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:20.638
So restarting Redis would have changed nothing, and taught us nothing.

76
00:03:20.638 --> 00:03:22.456
The problem is between the two.

77
00:03:23.133 --> 00:03:25.266
Check what the new Pod was given.

78
00:03:25.266 --> 00:03:28.238
printenv says rediss, with two S's at the end.

79
00:03:28.238 --> 00:03:29.660
It looks almost right.

80
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.789
We want to test that name from inside the Pod's network.

81
00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:36.424
But the app's image has no DNS tools.

82
00:03:36.424 --> 00:03:37.777
No nslookup at all.

83
00:03:37.933 --> 00:03:40.376
So we send a helper with a toolbox.

84
00:03:40.376 --> 00:03:47.355
kubectl debug adds a temporary container to the running Pod, with an image you choose, like busybox.

85
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:51.663
It shares the Pod's network, so its answers are the app's answers.

86
00:03:51.663 --> 00:03:54.416
Pause and guess: does the name rediss exist?

87
00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:57.283
NXDOMAIN.

88
00:03:57.283 --> 00:03:58.270
No such name.

89
00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:04.343
And the correct name, redis with one S, resolves to the Redis Service's address.

90
00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:05.732
That's the proof.

91
00:04:05.732 --> 00:04:08.906
A typo in the ConfigMap, one letter too many.

92
00:04:08.906 --> 00:04:11.093
Redis and the network are fine.

93
00:04:11.093 --> 00:04:14.267
One change fixes it, and we know exactly why.

94
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.423
That helper is called an ephemeral container: short-lived, never restarted, gone with the Pod.

95
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.117
A few guardrails.

96
00:04:23.117 --> 00:04:26.930
First, change one thing at a time, and watch what happens.

97
00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.231
That's the opposite of our manager.

98
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.542
Second, Forbidden is not a bug.

99
00:04:31.542 --> 00:04:34.099
Your key just doesn't open that door.

100
00:04:34.099 --> 00:04:36.033
Ask with kubectl auth can-i.

101
00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:41.206
Third, the honest limit: this finds causes only in what Kubernetes can see.

102
00:04:41.206 --> 00:04:45.344
Bugs in your own code need the app's logs, metrics and traces.

103
00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:49.376
The moral of the story: read before you touch.

104
00:04:49.376 --> 00:04:54.875
Climb from the Pod, to the Service, to the route, and the node when many fail.

105
00:04:54.875 --> 00:04:58.117
Get, describe, logs, events, then test inside.

106
00:04:58.117 --> 00:04:59.104
Never started?

107
00:04:59.104 --> 00:05:00.232
Read the Events.

108
00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:00.867
No tools?

109
00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:01.924
Bring your own.

110
00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:03.770
One quick check.

111
00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:05.424
A new Pod stays Pending.

112
00:05:05.424 --> 00:05:08.802
Its event says zero of three nodes are available.

113
00:05:08.802 --> 00:05:12.180
One had a taint, and two had insufficient memory.

114
00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:14.041
What's the most likely fix?

115
00:05:14.041 --> 00:05:15.144
Pause and think.

116
00:05:17.333 --> 00:05:20.095
Lower the memory request, or free up room.

117
00:05:20.095 --> 00:05:24.697
The scheduler counts requests, and the event accounted for every node.

118
00:05:24.697 --> 00:05:26.669
Read slowly, and it tells you.

119
00:05:26.669 --> 00:05:27.984
Thanks for watching.
